        <div id="spec-sheet" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. How to Read a Sub-Compact Spec Sheet Like-for-Like
          </h2>

          <p>
            Sub-compact spec sheets look comparable and are not, because manufacturers measure the same thing in different places. The three figures that matter most, loader lift, three-point lift and PTO horsepower, can each be quoted in a way that flatters the machine. Read the footnote under every number before comparing two tractors.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Loader Lift: Pivot Pin vs 500 mm Forward</h3>

          <p>
            Loader lift is the worst offender. The headline figure is usually measured at the pivot pin, the hinge where the bucket meets the arms, to full height. The bucket's center of gravity sits ahead of the pin, so the load you can actually lift in a bucket is lower. Deere publishes both for the 120R loader on the 1025R: 803 lb at the pivot pin and 553 lb at 500 mm forward. Published specs for Kubota's LA344 on the BX2680 show the same pattern, about 739 lb at the pin and about 509 lb at 500 mm. That 30 percent gap is the number to keep in mind when you look at a pile of wet gravel.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Three-Point Lift: Hitch Ends vs 24 Inches</h3>

          <p>
            Three-point lift has the same problem. A rating at the lift arm ends is always higher than a rating 24 inches behind them, which is where an implement's weight actually sits. LS publishes both for the MT125: 992 lb at the hitch ends and 728 lb at 24 inches. Deere quotes 758 lb at 24 inches for the 1025R; Kubota's BX2680 is listed around 680 lb at 24 inches. Mahindra's 1,323 lb for the eMAX 25L does not state a measuring point in the brochure, so ask the dealer before comparing it with the others.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Compare loader lift at 500 mm forward and 3-point lift at 24 inches</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Those are the figures closest to real working loads. If a dealer can only quote pivot-pin or lift-end numbers, ask for the others, or compare like-for-like only within the same measuring point.</p>
          </div>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">PTO Horsepower vs Engine Horsepower</h3>

          <p>
            PTO horsepower is the last one. Engine horsepower is gross output at the flywheel; PTO horsepower is what reaches the implement after the hydrostatic transmission takes its share, typically 70 to 80 percent of engine power in this class, though Kioti's rating for the CS2210 is higher. A 60-inch mid-mount deck, a snow blower or a rotary tiller is rated against PTO horsepower, so a tractor with 19.5 PTO hp will run a big deck in heavy grass with more reserve than one with 16.9 PTO hp, whatever the engine badges say.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="hitch" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Category 1 Hitch and the Limits of a Sub-Compact
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What Limited Category 1 Means</h3>

          <p>
            Every tractor here has a Category 1 three-point hitch, but several manufacturers call theirs limited Category 1, and Mahindra's smaller eMAX 20S uses Category 1 Narrow. Limited means the geometry and lift capacity are scaled to the tractor: standard Category 1 implements attach, but the tractor cannot lift or pull the heavier ones designed for 30 to 40 hp compact tractors.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Implements a Sub-Compact Can Run</h3>

          <p>
            In practice, a sub-compact runs a 4 to 5 foot box blade or land plane, a 4 foot rotary cutter for light brush, a 3 or 4 foot tiller, a post hole digger, a landscape rake, a rear blade and a snow blower. Buy implements sized and weighted for the class, and check the implement's minimum PTO horsepower against your tractor's PTO rating, not its engine rating.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where a Sub-Compact Runs Out</h3>

          <p>
            Where a sub-compact runs out is ground-engaging work in heavy soil and anything that needs weight to work. A box blade on hard clay, a 5-foot tiller in virgin sod or a rotary cutter in thick saplings will push a 1,500 lb tractor around. The fix inside the class is ballast: a weighted box on the hitch for loader work, filled rear tires, or wheel weights. The fix outside the class is a heavier compact tractor, and if most of your list is ground-engaging, the compact guide is the one to read.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="mower" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Mid-Mount Mower Decks: Drive-Over vs Hook-Up
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why the Belly Deck Matters</h3>

          <p>
            Most sub-compact owners mow with the tractor, so the belly deck deserves more attention than it gets in a showroom. Kubota, Deere, Mahindra and LS all list 60-inch mid-mount decks for these tractors (check the CS2210's deck options with a Kioti dealer). A 60-inch belly deck covers ground at roughly the same rate as a 60-inch zero turn at lower speed, and a sub-compact on turf tires leaves a cut that owners commonly describe as better than a lawn tractor's on uneven ground because of the tractor's weight and four-wheel drive.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Drive-Over vs Hook-Up Attachment</h3>

          <p>
            The practical difference is how the deck goes on and off. Deere's AutoConnect 54D and 60D decks on the 1025R attach by driving over the deck and lowering the lift, then engaging the mid PTO; owners commonly report a few minutes rather than a struggle on the garage floor. Other brands use hook-up designs that slide under the tractor and pin to the lift linkage. They work, but if you will swap between the deck and a loader or backhoe every weekend, the deck design is worth weighing as heavily as horsepower.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Loader Fit and Tire Choice</h3>

          <p>
            Two practical notes. First, a loader frame and a mid deck often coexist, but some combinations need the loader removed or a specific mounting kit for the deck to fit; ask the dealer to confirm both can live on the tractor together. Second, turf tires are the right choice for a tractor that mows a lawn, but they lose traction in mud and snow. Industrial (R4) tires are the common compromise for owners who mow and do loader work, and ag (R1) tires are for owners who rarely mow at all.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="loader" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Loader Work: What These Tractors Can Actually Lift
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What a Bucket of Material Weighs</h3>

          <p>
            A cubic yard of dry mulch weighs around 400 to 800 lb, a cubic yard of topsoil around 2,000 lb, and a cubic yard of gravel more than that. A sub-compact loader with 500 to 700 lb of real capacity at 500 mm forward therefore moves mulch in big scoops, topsoil in partial buckets, and gravel a third of a yard at a time. That is plenty for landscaping and a gravel drive. It is not a skid steer.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Loader Ratings Across These Tractors</h3>

          <p>
            The loader numbers on this page range from about 675 lb for the Kioti CS2210's SL2410 loader, per dealer listings, to 900 lb for the Mahindra eMAX 25L's 25LSK and 1,008 lb for the LS MT125, both at the manufacturer's quoted measuring point. If loader work is the main job, those differences matter more than horsepower. If mowing is the main job, they matter less than PTO output and the deck.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Rear Ballast Is Not Optional</h3>

          <p>
            Rear ballast is not optional with a loaded bucket. Lifting a heavy load on the front of a 1,500 lb tractor lightens the rear wheels, costs steering and in the worst case lets the tractor tip forward on a slope. A ballast box on the three-point hitch or loaded rear tires is part of using the loader safely. The quick-attach coupler is the other loader spec to check: Mahindra fits a skid-steer style coupler as standard, which opens up the aftermarket for forks, grapples and buckets at competitive prices.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="hst" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Hydrostatic Transmissions and Two-Range Drive
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why Hydrostatic Suits This Class</h3>

          <p>
            All five tractors use hydrostatic transmissions, and for this class that is the right answer. A hydrostatic drive has no clutch to slip and no gears to pick: you press a forward or reverse pedal and the tractor moves at a speed proportional to the pedal. For loader work, which is constant shuttling between forward and reverse, it is faster and less tiring than any gear drive, and it is far easier on turf than a clutch.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Using Low and High Range</h3>

          <p>
            Two-range means a low range for loader and implement work, where you want torque and fine control, and a high range for travel and mowing. Mahindra lists two ranges on the eMAX 25L HST, LS on the MT125, and Kioti on the CS2210. Learn to use low range for any loader work: it keeps the hydrostatic system cooler and gives you more pushing force into a pile.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Efficiency Trade-Off</h3>

          <p>
            The cost of a hydrostatic drive is efficiency: some of the engine's output is lost in the hydraulic pump and motor, which is part of why PTO horsepower sits below engine horsepower. That is also why a low-rpm, larger-displacement engine like Mahindra's 1,175cc three-cylinder can feel strong under load while showing a modest PTO figure on paper. Judge the PTO number for implements, and the loader numbers for material handling.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="dealer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Dealer-Only: How to Buy a Sub-Compact
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why Dealers, Not Amazon</h3>

          <p>
            Sub compact tractors are sold through dealers, not online retailers. You will not find the tractors on this page listed by the manufacturers on Amazon; listings that come up under their model numbers are filters, seat covers and parts. That is not a drawback, because a tractor needs pre-delivery setup, a dealer for warranty work, and someone who can supply a hydraulic hose on a Saturday.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Quote the Whole Package</h3>

          <p>
            Get quotes as packages: tractor, loader, mid-mount deck if you will mow, one or two rear implements, and ballast, all on one sheet with delivery. Compare them out the door, not by sticker. Manufacturer financing at low or zero percent is common in this class, and some dealers trade financing against price on the same quote, so negotiate the price first and the financing second.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Let Dealer Distance Break Ties</h3>

          <p>
            Dealer distance should break ties. A Kubota or Deere dealer is close to most buyers in the US; LS, Kioti and Mahindra networks are good in some regions and thin in others. A tractor you can get serviced 20 minutes away beats a slightly better spec sheet two hours away, and resale for Kubota and Deere sub-compacts is commonly strong, which narrows the price gap over the life of the machine.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. When a Sub Compact Tractor Is the Wrong Buy
          </h2>

          <p>
            Skip a sub-compact if your work is almost entirely mowing. A good zero turn mows faster, costs a fraction as much, and needs less maintenance. Rent a small tractor or skid steer for the two weekends a year you move material, and you will still be ahead after a decade.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip it if your list is mostly ground-engaging: tilling a large garden in clay, brush-hogging pasture, plowing, or grading with a heavy box blade. A sub-compact can do each job once; doing them every season wants the weight and full Category 1 hitch of a compact utility tractor. And skip it if you need to mow steep banks: a sub-compact's center of gravity rises with a loader on, and a slope that is fine for a walk mower is not fine for a tractor carrying a bucket.
          </p>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

