        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing loader lift at different measuring points</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A pivot-pin rating is always higher than one at 500 mm forward, often by about 30 percent. Deere's 120R is 803 lb at the pin and 553 lb at 500 mm. Compare tractors on the same measuring point, or the bigger number on the brochure may be the smaller real-world loader.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying on engine horsepower</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Implements are rated against PTO horsepower, which in this class runs roughly 70 to 80 percent of engine power. A 24 hp tractor with 16.9 PTO hp has less to give a 60-inch deck than a 24.8 hp tractor with 19.5. Ask for PTO output and compare that.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping rear ballast</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Lifting a full bucket on a 1,500 lb tractor without rear weight lightens the rear wheels and costs steering and stability. A ballast box on the three-point hitch or loaded rear tires is part of the loader, not an accessory to buy later.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing tires for the showroom, not the property</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Turf tires protect a lawn and lose grip in mud and snow; ag tires dig in and tear turf. If the tractor will mow and do loader work, industrial tires are the usual compromise. Decide before delivery, because swapping later is expensive.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring dealer distance</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A sub-compact is a twenty-year machine with hydraulics, filters and electronics. A dealer 20 minutes away who can supply parts and service beats a cheaper quote two hours away, and it usually shows up again in resale value.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
